1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,900
Last lecture ended on a warning worth taking seriously: good scorecard numbers don't

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:09,070
guarantee a good system, because the scorecard was run once, on a corpus that

3
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:10,099
doesn't stay still.

4
00:00:10,099 --> 00:00:14,540
Here are the six specific ways that gap actually shows up once real users and real

5
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:15,515
time get involved.

6
00:00:15,515 --> 00:00:18,819
Each one maps to a stage from the pipeline two lectures back.

7
00:00:18,819 --> 00:00:23,044
Knowing the name is what turns a vague "it's acting weird" into a specific bug

8
00:00:23,044 --> 00:00:24,777
report someone can actually fix.

9
00:00:24,777 --> 00:00:29,164
Notice, too, why this list matters even if you never write a line of the pipeline

10
00:00:29,164 --> 00:00:29,652
yourself.

11
00:00:29,652 --> 00:00:33,984
When something goes wrong in production, the room defaults to blaming the model,

12
00:00:33,984 --> 00:00:36,043
because the model is the visible part.

13
00:00:36,043 --> 00:00:38,101
But is the model actually the problem?

14
00:00:38,101 --> 00:00:41,784
Five of these six failures have nothing to do with the model at all.

15
00:00:41,784 --> 00:00:46,063
Naming the actual failure is how you stop a real bug from turning into a vague,

16
00:00:46,063 --> 00:00:48,771
unfixable complaint about the AI being unreliable.

17
00:00:49,321 --> 00:00:53,906
Read down that middle column, because every one of these looks completely different

18
00:00:53,906 --> 00:00:58,547
from the outside and traces back to a specific, nameable cause once you know what to

19
00:00:58,547 --> 00:00:59,044
look for.

20
00:00:59,044 --> 00:01:03,519
Two of these, staleness and permissions, are big enough to earn their own lecture

21
00:01:03,519 --> 00:01:05,453
next, so we'll only name them here.

22
00:01:05,453 --> 00:01:09,928
The other four are worth sitting with now, because they're easy to misdiagnose as

23
00:01:09,928 --> 00:01:14,568
"the model being unreliable" when the actual bug is sitting somewhere earlier in the

24
00:01:14,568 --> 00:01:15,066
pipeline.

25
00:01:15,066 --> 00:01:19,651
Context overflow deserves a second look, because the fix in that row isn't obvious.

26
00:01:19,651 --> 00:01:24,181
When retrieved chunks and conversation history together exceed the context window,

27
00:01:24,181 --> 00:01:28,601
something has to get cut, and most systems default to trimming whichever arrived

28
00:01:28,601 --> 00:01:30,590
last, usually the retrieved content.

29
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:31,529
That's backwards.

30
00:01:31,529 --> 00:01:34,678
History is often padding by the time it's five turns old.

31
00:01:34,678 --> 00:01:38,214
The retrieved chunks are the actual facts the answer depends on.

32
00:01:38,214 --> 00:01:41,639
Trim history first, and only touch retrieval as a last resort.

33
00:01:42,189 --> 00:01:46,934
Silent failure is the row worth the most attention of the four, because it's the one

34
00:01:46,934 --> 00:01:49,646
that produces the most convincing wrong answers.

35
00:01:49,646 --> 00:01:54,109
A model asked a question with nothing relevant in its retrieved context doesn't

36
00:01:54,109 --> 00:01:54,956
usually say so.

37
00:01:54,956 --> 00:01:59,645
It answers anyway, confidently, the exact hallucination mechanism from Section one.

38
00:01:59,645 --> 00:02:04,333
That gap is precisely what the degradation path from Section three exists to close.

39
00:02:04,883 --> 00:02:08,315
Remember Ticket 4471 from the context engineering lecture.

40
00:02:08,315 --> 00:02:13,108
That bug now has a name, and it's worth hearing it named formally, because you'll

41
00:02:13,108 --> 00:02:15,890
meet this exact shape again in your own system.

42
00:02:15,890 --> 00:02:20,742
Near-duplicate confusion happens whenever two versions of similar content both sit

43
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:25,712
in the index, both look relevant to the same query, and both get retrieved together.

44
00:02:25,712 --> 00:02:27,724
Neither one is individually wrong.

45
00:02:27,724 --> 00:02:31,393
The model, reading both, produces something blended and false.

46
00:02:31,393 --> 00:02:33,109
It isn't a reasoning failure.

47
00:02:33,109 --> 00:02:37,074
It's an index that was never cleaned of the document it superseded.

48
00:02:37,074 --> 00:02:41,571
Notice how this connects directly back to the honest caveat on chunking, two

49
00:02:41,571 --> 00:02:46,482
lectures ago: an embedding covering more than one idea matches worse than a focused

50
00:02:46,482 --> 00:02:46,719
one.

51
00:02:46,719 --> 00:02:51,334
Two near-duplicate chunks, retrieved side by side, do something similar to the

52
00:02:51,334 --> 00:02:52,400
model's reasoning.

53
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:57,015
It's now holding two versions of the truth at once, with nothing in the prompt

54
00:02:57,015 --> 00:02:58,909
telling it which one is current.

55
00:02:59,459 --> 00:03:04,192
Say the honest caveat, because a RAG system is unusual in exactly this way: the code

56
00:03:04,192 --> 00:03:06,953
can stay frozen and the system can still degrade.

57
00:03:06,953 --> 00:03:09,995
A normal feature breaks when someone changes the code.

58
00:03:09,995 --> 00:03:14,728
A RAG system can break when someone on a completely different team uploads a revised

59
00:03:14,728 --> 00:03:19,180
policy document and forgets to remove the old one, six months after your launch

60
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:20,476
review closed the file.

61
00:03:20,476 --> 00:03:22,053
Nobody touched the pipeline.

62
00:03:22,053 --> 00:03:26,505
The corpus underneath it quietly grew a near-duplicate, and your scorecard from

63
00:03:26,505 --> 00:03:27,801
launch day has no idea.

64
00:03:27,801 --> 00:03:32,308
That's the argument for treating retrieval quality the way Section four treats a

65
00:03:32,308 --> 00:03:36,985
golden set: something you re-check on a schedule, not something you verify once and

66
00:03:36,985 --> 00:03:37,999
consider finished.

67
00:03:37,999 --> 00:03:42,732
A PRD that specifies a threshold and never revisits it has quietly assumed the world

68
00:03:42,732 --> 00:03:43,408
holds still.

69
00:03:43,408 --> 00:03:45,831
This section keeps proving that it doesn't.

70
00:03:46,381 --> 00:03:50,708
That's the concept worth carrying past this lecture: the pipeline can be perfect and

71
00:03:50,708 --> 00:03:55,035
the system can still drift, because the documents underneath it have a life of their

72
00:03:55,035 --> 00:03:55,241
own.

73
00:03:55,241 --> 00:03:56,838
The exercise takes ten minutes.

74
00:03:56,838 --> 00:03:59,878
Search for the same topic twice in your own knowledge base.

75
00:03:59,878 --> 00:04:03,999
If two documents come back covering it, you've found this lecture's failure mode

76
00:04:03,999 --> 00:04:07,501
sitting live in your own system, not a warning about someone else's.

77
00:04:08,051 --> 00:04:09,703
One thing before you move on.

78
00:04:09,703 --> 00:04:10,557
Run the search.

79
00:04:10,557 --> 00:04:15,282
One topic, two documents, is often all it takes to find your first real instance of

80
00:04:15,282 --> 00:04:16,706
near-duplicate confusion.

81
00:04:16,706 --> 00:04:21,261
Then ask the question most launches never answer: who's actually responsible for

82
00:04:21,261 --> 00:04:24,677
pulling a document out of the index once it's been replaced.

83
00:04:24,677 --> 00:04:29,289
If the honest answer is nobody, that's the gap this whole section keeps returning

84
00:04:29,289 --> 00:04:32,648
to, an unowned decision quietly becoming someone's Tuesday.

85
00:04:32,648 --> 00:04:37,146
Next lecture goes deep on the two failures we only named today: permissions and

86
00:04:37,146 --> 00:04:41,929
freshness, the two realities that break more enterprise RAG demos than anything else

87
00:04:41,929 --> 00:04:42,669
on this list.
